# CODEOWNERS — the gate must not be editable by the MR it judges.
#
# Every file that decides the verdict (the scripts, the scanner rules, the
# hook config, the CI job definitions, this file) needs an approval from the
# gate's owner before it merges. Without this, the party under check can
# narrow a regex or widen an allowlist in the same MR — and the deterministic
# layer stops being independent of the LLM layer, which is the whole point.
#
# Replace @OWNER with a team or user (GitHub: @org/team or @user; GitLab:
# @group or @user). Then make the review REQUIRED — the file alone only
# requests it (see ci/README.md → Protected-branch):
#   GitHub  branch protection → required_pull_request_reviews.require_code_owner_reviews
#   GitLab  protected branch → code_owner_approval_required (Premium; on Free this
#           file assigns reviewers but enforces nothing — say so in your docs)
# Solo repo: the author cannot approve their own MR — either a second account
# owns the gate, or keep the file for visibility and skip enforcement, knowingly.
#
# Copied from the task-flow `ci-gate` payload; read by GitHub and GitLab from
# the repository root.

/ci/                      @OWNER
/.gitleaks.toml           @OWNER
/.pre-commit-config.yaml  @OWNER
# .gitattributes decides what git shows as text: `*.js -diff` hides a file from
# the local `gitleaks protect --staged` hook (it has no --text override). The CI
# scans, pre-push and unicode-guard pass --text and are not blinded — but the
# attribute still changes what every reviewer's `git diff` shows.
# No leading slash: git reads a .gitattributes from every directory, so the
# rule has to match the file wherever it appears.
.gitattributes            @OWNER
# All three places a CODEOWNERS file is read from — otherwise a change can add
# a higher-priority one that owns nothing (GitHub reads .github/ first, then
# the root, then docs/; GitLab reads the root, then .gitlab/, then docs/).
/CODEOWNERS               @OWNER
/.github/CODEOWNERS       @OWNER
/.gitlab/CODEOWNERS       @OWNER
/docs/CODEOWNERS          @OWNER
# The whole workflows directory, not just gate.yml: a required status check is
# matched by NAME, so a new workflow declaring a job called `secret-scan` can
# report a green check under the required name.
/.github/workflows/       @OWNER
# GitLab: the project file carries the gate's `include:` — own it too
/.gitlab-ci.yml           @OWNER
# Optionally add your migrations dirs (the marker in a migration is a
# declaration by its author, not an approval by anyone else):
# /db/migrate/            @OWNER
